Build **Meltdown**, an open-field tower-defense game with a heat-as-power combat
system that runs entirely in the browser.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Create the game there.

## Your starting point

A starter project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`: a `package.json` that
pins **Playwright** as a dev dependency. Its dependencies have already been
installed for you — `node_modules/` is populated and Playwright's headless
**Chromium** is downloaded — so you can build and verify right away. Build on this
project rather than replacing it: keep the `package.json`, add your framework,
bundler, and a `build` script on top of it, and keep the lockfile in sync (see
below).

## What to read first

The full specification is split across the files below, all already present in
your project. Read every one of them before you start, then implement the game
they describe as a single, cohesive build:

{{#each specs}}
- `{{this.path}}`
{{/each}}

Start with `overview` for the goals, hard requirements, coordinate system, and
palette. Then read `heat` carefully — heat as power, the accelerating
damage curve, the redline trip, and the Forge/Vent coupling are the heart of this
game. Then `playfield` (the tile floor, the intakes and exhausts, towers-as-walls
mazing, and how the surge paths), `towers` (the six emitters and two movers, their
stats and thermal stances, upgrades, and selling), `creeps` (the surge types and
the maze-ignoring flyers), `controls` (mouse-driven building, selecting, and
running the waves), and `flow` (the economy, lives, the 20-wave progression and
victory, scoring, and states). Finally read the mode spec(s) under `modes/` for
the playable modes. The specs cross-reference each other by name — treat them as
one specification. **There is no fixed maze: the player builds it at runtime, so
do not ship one** — you design the surge's spawn timing and per-wave composition
within the constraints in `creeps` and `flow`.

## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. It must:

- **Render real graphics** (Canvas 2D, WebGL/WebGPU, or positioned DOM) — not a
  text-only or ASCII rendering.
- **Run in the browser with no backend** — no server, accounts, database, or
  network calls at runtime; everything needed to play is self-contained.
- Require **no API keys or credentials** of any kind to build, run, or play.
- Stay a **Node project** built with only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies —
  no separately installed language toolchain. Extend the provided `package.json`
  at `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run `npm install` after
  you change dependencies so the lockfile matches) — the build is installed with
  `npm ci`, which requires it. `npm ci` then `npm run build` must produce the
  static site, with no other manual step, into one of `dist/`, `build/`, or
  `out/` at the project root, with an `index.html` at the root of that directory.
  Serving that directory as-is at the root of any static file server must run the
  game; it is deployed to static hosting exactly that way.
- Include a `README.md` at `{{workspace}}` explaining what the game is, how to
  install dependencies, how to run it in development, how to produce the static
  production build, and the controls.

You choose the language, framework, bundler, and rendering approach within those
constraints — only the `npm ci` / `npm run build` commands and their
`dist`/`build`/`out` output are fixed (plain TypeScript with Canvas 2D is entirely
sufficient). The `reference/` folder under `{{workspace}}` holds screenshots that
are visual targets for the title, gameplay, and game-over screens — match their
layout, palette, and type, but build the screens from the specification rather
than copying the images, and remember the maze, towers, and surge they show are
just one example moment.

## Verify before you finish

Your project has **Playwright** and a headless **Chromium** installed as project
dependencies, plus network access — use them to verify your build, do not just
assume it works. Before you consider the task complete:

- Produce the **static production build** and serve it locally, then open it in
  Chromium with Playwright and confirm it **loads with no console errors** and no
  uncaught exceptions.
- **Drive the game** — build a maze of towers, watch the surge re-path around them,
  send waves, let an emitter heat up and trip the redline, brake one with a Vent
  and warm one with a Forge, shoot down a flyer with a Flak, take leaks to a loss
  and (if you can) clear waves toward victory — and **screenshot** the title,
  gameplay, and end screens. Compare each against the matching image in
  `reference/` and against the specs, and fix what does not match before finishing.
- **Capture the proof of implementation** exactly as `specs/proof.md` specifies:
  the screenshots and the two short clips (`heat.mp4`, `siege.mp4`), each written
  to the precise `proof/` path that spec names. These are required deliverables of
  the finished build, not a private check. Do not invent a different folder or
  filename, and do not skip the `.mp4` clips.

Use the project's own Playwright: import it from your project (for example
`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the project-local CLI with
`npx playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on `PATH` — it lives in your
project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency. Launch Chromium with the
`--no-sandbox` argument — this container has no Chromium sandbox, so a default
launch will fail.
